<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anouvong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Chao Anouvong, Chao Anou, Xaiya Setthathirath V</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anouvong was the last king of Vientiane, ruling from 1805 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Siam. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-si-saket">Wat Si Saket</a> and other monuments and sought to restore Lao independence. In 1826 he rebelled and marched towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, but his armies were defeated, Vientiane was sacked in 1827 and its people deported. Captured in 1828, he died a prisoner in Bangkok, and he is honoured as a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anouvong.jpg" alt="Anouvong" width="229" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chaoborus · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StadAnouvong-entrance-2011_(version_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anouvong was a son of King Siribounyasan of Vientiane. When the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> of the Thonburi Kingdom took Vientiane in 1779, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a> was carried off, the king's sons and daughter were taken as hostages, and several thousand Lao families were resettled at Saraburi. His brothers Nanthasen and Inthavong reigned before him. As oupahat, or vice-king, under Inthavong, he led Lao armies to help Siam against the Burmese invasions of 1797 and 1802 and won several victories at Sipsong Chao Thai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>From 1813 he made religious and symbolic gestures that remain controversial: he called a great council of the sangha, which decided to carve a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a>, repaired the Ho Phra Kèo and founded new temples. In 1819 his son Nyô put down a revolt in the Kingdom of Champasak and was made its ruler by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a>, bringing two of the three Lao kingdoms under the family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-si-saket">Wat Si Saket</a>, finished in 1824, was oriented so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> pledging allegiance turned their backs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>.</p><p>At the funeral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a>, Anouvong's retinue and a son were put to forced labour, and he asked in vain for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a>, his sister and the Saraburi families. Thai historians say he rebelled over a personal slight; the scale of the rising suggests more complex motives. In December 1826 his armies entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Khorat Plateau</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-ratchasima">Nakhon Ratchasima</a> by a ruse and moving Lao people north. Siam, armed with modern British firearms, struck back, took the fortress of Nong Bua Lamphu after three days and then Vientiane.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Anouvong returned with about a thousand soldiers to negotiate, but enraged by a Siamese victory stupa at Yasothon he attacked its garrison. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> ordered Vientiane destroyed. Pursued to Xieng Khouang, Anouvong was, by some accounts, betrayed by Chao Noy. A British observer quoted by Sir John Bowring described him held in an iron cage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, where he soon died. Vientiane was razed, and French explorers more than 30 years later found only ruins in a jungle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Khorat Plateau</a> was annexed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> explain why over 19 million ethnic Lao now live in Isan. Lao nationalists honour him, and Chao Anouvong Park, with his bronze statue, opened in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anouvong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the failed rising of <b>Chao Anouvong</b> of Vientiane in 1826–1828, the Siamese sacked his capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Chiang Mai was secured as a vassal and bulwark against Burma, and when <b>Anouvong</b> of Vientiane rebelled in 1826 and marched towards Bangkok, Siamese forces drove him back, sacked Vientiane and captured him; he died in captivity in Bangkok.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Anouvong</b> built Wat Si Saket and tried to restore Lao power.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vientiane">Kingdom of Vientiane</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vientiane, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.98,102.63&amp;year=1807" aria-label="Vientiane on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7334 1497 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anouvong"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7784 1767h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7784 1767h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.98,102.63&amp;year=1807">What was here in 1807? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
